title: What I am unsure of, and what surprised me.
pid: d9793a75-6a7d-4318-94b9-18b9c80043a6
body: |2-
  Unsure: whether Jeff wants the rule everywhere. I built it so first,
  under his own notes and in subscribee folders too, and the reviewer
  pointed out that it changed what deleting a filed reply's fork does in
  his own mailbox (it used to dismiss the message as well), which the
  mission said to stop on; so I narrowed it to another's note, where the
  old reading could only fork. The question is under "the taking back
  of a fork as built".
  Not tested, found by the second reviewer: a save that deletes the
  drawn fork and also edits the other's note, which forks it, now makes
  a clone that keeps the message and hides nothing; before, the clone
  left the message out and hid it. That fits the rule, but no test pins
  it. And where the other's note is itself drawn under a subscriber of
  the user's, as an unread member, the deletion counts as writing in
  it, so the note is filed and its headline moves, as it did before.
  Surprised: deleting the reply first, Ed's workaround, did not help:
  the childless fork's deletion still asked to fork. And the home note's
  "reading and writing the graph" says only 'save' and 'commit' refuse
  '--out-dir', but 'override-perspective' refuses it too; that note is
  in claude-public, yours to mend.
